\label{ch:heterogeneous}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:hetero-intro}

Modern computing systems are increasingly \emph{heterogeneous}: a single system may contain CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, tensor processing units (TPUs), neural processing units (NPUs), and other specialized accelerators. Each device has different capabilities, programming models, and performance characteristics. The compiler's task is to partition the computation across these devices, generate code for each, and manage the communication between them.

This chapter covers the challenges and techniques of heterogeneous compilation, from multi-target IR design and device placement to GPU compilation, FPGA synthesis, and memory management across devices.

\section{Multi-Target IR Design}\label{sec:multi-target-ir}

\subsection{The Challenge}\label{sec:multi-target-challenge}

A multi-target IR must represent computations in a way that is independent of any specific device, while still capturing enough information for device-specific code generation.

\begin{definition}[Multi-Target IR]\label{def:multi-target-ir}
A \emph{multi-target IR} is an intermediate representation that can be lowered to code for multiple target devices (CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, accelerators) from a single representation.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Design Principles}\label{sec:multi-target-principles}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Device abstraction}: the IR should not assume a specific device architecture.
  \item \textbf{Parallelism representation}: the IR must express data parallelism, task parallelism, and pipeline parallelism.
  \item \textbf{Memory hierarchy}: the IR must represent different memory spaces (global, shared, local, constant).
  \item \textbf{Synchronization}: the IR must express barriers, atomics, and communication patterns.
  \item \textbf{Lowering paths}: the IR must support systematic lowering to each target.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Examples of Multi-Target IRs}\label{sec:multi-target-examples}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{MLIR}: LLVM's Multi-Level IR supports multiple levels of abstraction, from high-level tensor operations to low-level device-specific code. Dialects like \texttt{gpu}, \texttt{spirv}, \texttt{nvvm}, and \texttt{rocdl} target different GPU architectures.
  \item \textbf{Halide}: a language and compiler for image processing that targets CPUs, GPUs, and specialized hardware.
  \item \textbf{Apache TVM}: a deep learning compiler that targets CPUs, GPUs, and accelerators.
  \item \textbf{OpenCL/SYCL}: portable parallel programming models that compile to multiple devices.
\end{itemize}

\section{Device Placement and Scheduling}\label{sec:placement}

\subsection{The Placement Problem}\label{sec:placement-problem}

\begin{definition}[Device Placement]\label{def:device-placement}
\emph{Device placement} is the problem of assigning each computation (operation, function, or subgraph) to a specific device in a heterogeneous system, such that the overall execution time (including communication) is minimized.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Formulation}\label{sec:placement-formulation}

The placement problem can be formulated as an optimization problem:

\begin{definition}[Placement Optimization]\label{def:placement-opt}
Given:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A computation graph $G = (V, E)$ where nodes are operations and edges are data dependencies.
  \item A set of devices $D = \{d_1, \ldots, d_k\}$ with different capabilities and costs.
  \item A cost function $\text{cost}(v, d)$ for executing operation $v$ on device $d$.
  \item A communication cost $\text{comm}(e, d_i, d_j)$ for transferring data along edge $e$ between devices $d_i$ and $d_j$.
\end{itemize}
Find an assignment $A: V \to D$ that minimizes:
\[
  \sum_{v \in V} \text{cost}(v, A(v)) + \sum_{(u,v) \in E} \text{comm}((u,v), A(u), A(v))
\]
\end{definition}

This is an NP-hard problem in general, but practical heuristics work well:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Greedy placement}: place each operation on the device that minimizes its local cost.
  \item \textbf{Graph partitioning}: partition the computation graph to minimize cross-partition edges.
  \item \textbf{RL-based placement}: use reinforcement learning to learn placement policies.
  \item \textbf{Placer}: Google's ML-based placer for TensorFlow, which uses path-based grouping and cost modeling.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Profiling for Placement}\label{sec:placement-profiling}

Accurate placement requires profiling data:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Measure the execution time of each operation on each device.
  \item Measure the communication cost between each pair of devices.
  \item Use these measurements to build the cost model for placement.
\end{itemize}

\section{GPU Compilation}\label{sec:gpu-compilation}

\subsection{GPU Architecture Overview}\label{sec:gpu-arch}

GPUs have a fundamentally different architecture from CPUs:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Massive parallelism}: thousands of cores (e.g., NVIDIA A100 has 6912 CUDA cores).
  \item \textbf{SIMT execution}: groups of threads (warps/wavefronts) execute the same instruction on different data.
  \item \textbf{Memory hierarchy}: global memory (large, slow), shared memory (small, fast, per-block), registers (per-thread).
  \item \textbf{Thread hierarchy}: threads $\to$ warps $\to$ thread blocks $\to$ grid.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{GPU Programming Models}\label{sec:gpu-programs}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{CUDA}: NVIDIA's programming model. Kernels are functions executed by threads organized in blocks.
  \item \textbf{OpenCL}: a portable standard for heterogeneous parallel programming.
  \item \textbf{SYCL}: a C++ abstraction layer over OpenCL.
  \item \textbf{HIP}: AMD's GPU programming model, API-compatible with CUDA.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compilation Pipeline}\label{sec:gpu-pipeline}

The GPU compilation pipeline typically involves:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Kernel extraction}: identify parallel regions (loops, map operations) that can be compiled as GPU kernels.
  \item \textbf{Parallelism mapping}: map the program's parallelism to the GPU's thread hierarchy (threads, blocks, grid dimensions).
  \item \textbf{Memory management}: allocate data to different memory spaces (global, shared, registers).
  \item \textbf{Synchronization insertion}: add barriers and memory fences as needed.
  \item \textbf{Code generation}: generate PTX (NVIDIA), AMDGCN (AMD), or SPIR-V (portable) code.
  \item \textbf{Assembly and linking}: assemble and link the GPU code into the final binary.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Loop-to-Kernel Transformation}\label{sec:loop-to-kernel}

A key transformation is converting sequential loops into GPU kernels:

\begin{example}[Loop to GPU Kernel]\label{ex:loop-to-gpu}
Source code:
\begin{verbatim}
  for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    C[i] = A[i] + B[i];
\end{verbatim}
Transformed to CUDA kernel:
\begin{verbatim}
  __global__ void add_kernel(float* A, float* B, float* C, int N) {
      int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
      if (i < N)
          C[i] = A[i] + B[i];
  }
  // Launch: add_kernel<<<(N+255)/256, 256>>>(A, B, C, N);
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{GPU-Specific Optimizations}\label{sec:gpu-optimizations}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Coalesced memory access}: arrange data access patterns so that threads in a warp access contiguous memory.
  \item \textbf{Shared memory tiling}: load data into shared memory to reduce global memory traffic.
  \item \textbf{Warp-level primitives}: use shuffle, vote, and match instructions for intra-warp communication.
  \item \textbf{Occupancy tuning}: balance register usage and block size to maximize GPU occupancy.
\end{itemize}

\section{FPGA Compilation}\label{sec:fpga-compilation}

\subsection{FPGA Architecture}\label{sec:fpga-arch}

FPGAs (Field-Programmable Gate Arrays) are reconfigurable hardware:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Consist of configurable logic blocks (CLBs), routing resources, and I/O blocks.
  \item Can implement custom hardware pipelines with deterministic latency.
  \item Offer higher performance-per-watt than CPUs/GPUs for suitable workloads.
  \item Have higher programming complexity and longer compilation times.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{High-Level Synthesis (HLS)}\label{sec:hls}

\begin{definition}[High-Level Synthesis]\label{def:hls}
\emph{High-level synthesis} (HLS) is the process of converting a high-level program (typically in C, C++, or SystemC) into hardware description language (HDL) code (Verilog or VHDL) that can be synthesized onto an FPGA.
\end{definition}

The HLS pipeline:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Scheduling}: assign operations to clock cycles.
  \item \textbf{Binding}: assign operations to hardware resources (ALUs, multipliers, memories).
  \item \textbf{Allocation}: determine the number of each type of resource needed.
  \item \textbf{RTL generation}: generate Verilog/VHDL from the scheduled and bound design.
  \item \textbf{Synthesis}: convert RTL to FPGA-specific configuration.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{FPGA Compilation from IR}\label{sec:fpga-from-ir}

Modern approaches compile from a multi-target IR to FPGA:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{CIRCT}: an LLVM-based circuit IR and toolset for hardware design.
  \item \textbf{Intel HLS}: compiles C++ to FPGA via HLS.
  \item \textbf{Xilinx Vitis}: compiles OpenCL/C/C++ to FPGA.
  \item \textbf{MLIR-based HLS}: uses MLIR dialects to represent hardware at multiple levels.
\end{itemize}

\section{Accelerator Integration}\label{sec:accelerator}

\subsection{Specialized Accelerators}\label{sec:specialized}

Modern systems include various specialized accelerators:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)}: matrix multiplication accelerators for neural networks.
  \item \textbf{Neural Processing Units (NPUs)}: accelerators for inference workloads.
  \item \textbf{Digital Signal Processors (DSPs)}: optimized for signal processing.
  \item \textbf{Video codecs}: hardware encoders/decoders (H.264, H.265, AV1).
  \item \textbf{Cryptography accelerators}: AES, SHA, RSA hardware.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compiler Support for Accelerators}\label{sec:accel-support}

The compiler must:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Detect} patterns that match the accelerator's capabilities.
  \item \textbf{Lower} the computation to the accelerator's programming model.
  \item \textbf{Manage} data transfer between the host and the accelerator.
  \item \textbf{Schedule} operations to overlap computation and communication.
\end{itemize}

\section{Memory Management Across Devices}\label{sec:memory-management}

\subsection{The Multi-Device Memory Problem}\label{sec:multi-device-memory}

In a heterogeneous system, each device has its own memory:
\begin{itemize}
  \item CPU: main memory (DRAM), caches (L1/L2/L3).
  \item GPU: device memory (HBM/GDDR), shared memory, registers.
  \item FPGA: on-chip BRAM, external DDR.
  \item Accelerator: device-specific memory.
\end{itemize}

Data must be explicitly transferred between devices, which is expensive.

\subsection{Unified Memory}\label{sec:unified-memory}

\begin{definition}[Unified Memory]\label{def:unified-memory}
\emph{Unified memory} is a memory model where a single address space is shared across all devices. The hardware (or runtime) automatically migrates data between devices as needed.
\end{definition}

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{CUDA Unified Memory}: \texttt{cudaMallocManaged} allocates memory accessible from both CPU and GPU.
  \item \textbf{HSA (Heterogeneous System Architecture)}: defines a unified address space for CPUs and GPUs.
  \item \textbf{CXL (Compute Express Link)}: a hardware protocol for memory sharing between devices.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Explicit Memory Management}\label{sec:explicit-memory}

Without unified memory, the compiler must insert explicit data transfers:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Multi-Device Memory Management}
\label{alg:multi-device-memory}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\For{each computation $c$ assigned to device $d$}
  \For{each input $x$ of $c$}
    \If{$x$ is not in device $d$'s memory}
      \State \textsc{Transfer}($x$, from $x$.\text{current\_device} to $d$)
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State Execute $c$ on device $d$
  \For{each output $y$ of $c$}
    \State $y$.\text{current\_device} $\gets d$
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Memory Optimization}\label{sec:memory-optimization}

The compiler can optimize data transfers:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Transfer coalescing}: combine multiple small transfers into one large transfer.
  \item \textbf{Transfer elimination}: avoid unnecessary transfers by keeping data on the device that produces it.
  \item \textbf{Prefetching}: initiate transfers early to overlap with computation.
  \item \textbf{Pinning}: allocate memory that is accessible from multiple devices without copying.
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced GPU Compilation}\label{sec:advanced-gpu}

\subsection{Thread Coarsening and Grid Stratification}\label{sec:thread-coarsening}

A key GPU optimization is \emph{thread coarsening}: each thread processes multiple elements, reducing the overhead of thread management and improving register reuse.

\begin{definition}[Thread Coarsening]\label{def:thread-coarsening}
\emph{Thread coarsening} is the transformation of a GPU kernel so that each thread processes multiple data elements, reducing the total number of threads and increasing per-thread work.
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Before coarsening (one element per thread):
  __global__ void add(float* A, float* B, float* C, int N) {
      int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
      if (i < N) C[i] = A[i] + B[i];
  }
  
  ; After coarsening (4 elements per thread):
  __global__ void add_coarsened(float* A, float* B, float* C, int N) {
      int base = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) * 4;
      #pragma unroll
      for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
          int i = base + k;
          if (i < N) C[i] = A[i] + B[i];
      }
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Shared Memory Tiling}\label{sec:shared-memory-tiling}

For compute-intensive kernels, data is loaded into shared memory and reused across threads:

\begin{example}[Matrix Multiplication with Shared Memory Tiling]\label{ex:matmul-tiling}
\begin{verbatim}
  __global__ void matmul_tiled(float* A, float* B, float* C, int N) {
      __shared__ float As[TILE_SIZE][TILE_SIZE];
      __shared__ float Bs[TILE_SIZE][TILE_SIZE];
      
      int tx = threadIdx.x, ty = threadIdx.y;
      int row = blockIdx.y * TILE_SIZE + ty;
      int col = blockIdx.x * TILE_SIZE + tx;
      float sum = 0.0f;
      
      for (int t = 0; t < N / TILE_SIZE; t++) {
          As[ty][tx] = A[row * N + t * TILE_SIZE + tx];
          Bs[ty][tx] = B[(t * TILE_SIZE + ty) * N + col];
          __syncthreads();
          
          for (int k = 0; k < TILE_SIZE; k++)
              sum += As[ty][k] * Bs[k][tx];
          __syncthreads();
      }
      C[row * N + col] = sum;
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Warp-Level Optimizations}\label{sec:warp-optimizations}

Modern GPUs provide warp-level primitives that enable communication without shared memory:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Warp shuffle}: exchange values between threads in a warp without going through shared memory.
  \item \textbf{Warp vote}: perform reduction operations (any, all, majority) across a warp.
  \item \textbf{Warp match}: find threads in a warp with matching values.
  \item \textbf{Cooperative groups}: explicit thread group management for sub-warp and multi-warp synchronization.
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced FPGA Compilation}\label{sec:advanced-fpga}

\subsection{Pipeline Parallelism on FPGAs}\label{sec:fpga-pipeline}

FPGAs can implement deeply pipelined computations where different stages of a computation execute concurrently on different data elements:

\begin{definition}[Hardware Pipeline]\label{def:hw-pipeline}
A \emph{hardware pipeline} is a sequence of combinational logic stages separated by registers. Each stage processes a different data element, enabling throughput of one result per clock cycle after the pipeline is filled.
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Source loop:
  for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    C[i] = A[i] * B[i] + D[i];
  
  ; HLS-generated pipeline (3 stages):
  ; Stage 1: load A[i], B[i], D[i]   (1 cycle)
  ; Stage 2: multiply A[i] * B[i]     (1 cycle)
  ; Stage 3: add result + D[i], store  (1 cycle)
  ;
  ; After pipeline fill (3 cycles), throughput = 1 result/cycle
  ; Total latency = N + 2 cycles (vs. 3*N for unpipelined)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Loop Transformations for FPGAs}\label{sec:fpga-loops}

The compiler applies loop transformations to maximize FPGA utilization:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Loop pipelining}: overlap iterations of a loop so that multiple iterations execute concurrently.
  \item \textbf{Loop unrolling}: replicate the loop body to process multiple iterations per clock cycle.
  \item \textbf{Loop interchange}: reorder nested loops to enable pipelining of the inner loop.
  \item \textbf{Loop fusion}: merge adjacent loops to reduce memory traffic.
  \item \textbf{Loop tiling}: partition loops into blocks that fit in on-chip memory.
\end{itemize}

\section{Device Placement: Advanced Strategies}\label{sec:advanced-placement}

\subsection{Cost Model for Placement}\label{sec:placement-cost-model}

Accurate device placement requires a cost model that captures:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Cost model components for device placement}
\label{tab:placement-cost}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
Compute cost & Time to execute the operation on each device \\
Memory cost & Time to allocate and manage memory on each device \\
Communication cost & Time to transfer data between devices \\
Startup cost & Fixed overhead of launching a kernel on a device \\
Power cost & Energy consumed by executing on each device \\
Contention cost & Increased cost when multiple operations share a device \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Dynamic Device Placement}\label{sec:dynamic-placement}

Static placement (decided at compile time) may not be optimal because:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Input sizes may vary at runtime.
  \item Device availability may change (e.g., a GPU is busy with another task).
  \item Data-dependent control flow may change the computation graph.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Dynamic Device Placement]\label{def:dynamic-placement}
\emph{Dynamic device placement} decides at runtime which device executes each operation, based on the current state of the system (device load, data location, input sizes).
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Dynamic Device Placement}
\label{alg:dynamic-placement}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{DynamicPlace}{$\text{operation}, \text{system\_state}$}
  \For{each device $d$ in available\_devices}
    \Let{\text{compute}}{\text{estimated\_compute}(operation, d)}
    \Let{\text{comm}}{\text{estimated\_transfer}(operation.inputs, d)}
    \Let{\text{contention}}{\text{current\_load}(d)}
    \State $\text{total\_cost}(d) \gets \text{compute} + \text{comm} + \text{contention}$
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $\text{argmin}_d \; \text{total\_cost}(d)$
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Multi-Chip and Multi-Node Compilation}\label{sec:multi-chip}

\subsection{Distributed Compilation}\label{sec:distributed-compilation}

When computation spans multiple chips or nodes, the compiler must:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Partition} the computation graph across devices.
  \item \textbf{Schedule} operations to respect data dependencies and communication costs.
  \item \textbf{Generate} communication code (RPC, message passing, DMA transfers).
  \item \textbf{Overlap} computation and communication to hide latency.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Collective Communication Patterns}\label{sec:collective-comm}

Multi-device systems use collective communication patterns:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{All-reduce}: combine values from all devices (e.g., gradient aggregation in distributed training).
  \item \textbf{All-gather}: collect data from all devices to every device.
  \item \textbf{Reduce-scatter}: reduce and distribute fragments to different devices.
  \item \textbf{Broadcast}: send data from one device to all others.
\end{itemize}

The compiler must select the optimal collective algorithm (ring, tree, recursive halving-doubling) based on the network topology and message size.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:hetero-summary}

Heterogeneous compilation is one of the most challenging frontiers in compiler design. The compiler must partition computation across diverse devices, generate device-specific code, manage data transfers, and optimize for the unique characteristics of each device. As heterogeneous systems become the norm---from smartphones with CPUs, GPUs, and NPUs to data centers with CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, and custom accelerators---the importance of effective heterogeneous compilation will only grow.

\begin{historical}
Heterogeneous computing has evolved from early coprocessor designs (floating-point units, DSPs) through GPU computing (CUDA, 2007; OpenCL, 2009) to the modern era of diverse accelerators (TPUs, 2016; NPUs, FPGAs in data centers). The compilation challenges have grown accordingly: from simple offloading (sending a kernel to the GPU) to complex multi-device orchestration (partitioning a neural network across CPUs, GPUs, and TPUs). Frameworks like Halide (2013), TVM (2018), and MLIR (2019) represent the state of the art in multi-target compilation, providing abstractions that enable portable, high-performance code across diverse hardware.
\end{historical}
